e agent could potentially execute malicious commands, generate security vulnerabilities, or inadvertently access sensitive data.\n\nRed-teaming—the process of systematically probing systems to identify vulnerabilities—has emerged as a crucial methodology for evaluating code agents. Specialized red-teaming agents can simulate adversarial behavior, testing code agents against a variety of attack vectors including prompt injection, jailbreaking, and exploit generation. Understanding both the capabilities of code agents and the effectiveness of red-teaming methodologies is essential for developing safer AI systems.\n\nThis paper provides a comprehensive review of research advances in code agents and red-teaming. We examine the architectural foundations of modern code agents, review established red-teaming methodologies, and analyze recent frameworks specifically designed for testing code agent security. Our goal is to synthesize knowledge from multiple domains—including deep learning, natural language processing, and cybersecurity—to provide a holistic perspective on this rapidly evolving field.\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Code Agent Architectures\n\nModern code agents build upon foundational neural network architectures that have revolutionized deep learning. The Transformer architecture [1], which replaced recurrence and convolution with attention mechanisms, has become the backbone of most modern language models. For visual components often incorporated in code agents that process diagrams or user interfaces, residual learning frameworks [2] have proven essential for training very deep networks effectively.\n\nRecent work has extended these foundations to create specialized code agents. Building on the general-purpose capabilities of LLMs, researchers have developed architectures that combine code understanding with execution environments, enabling agents to write, test, and debug code autonomously. These systems leverage both the sequential processing capabilities of 