 Directions\n\nThe intersection of these research areas reveals important opportunities for advancing code agent security. Combining the trustworthiness evaluation frameworks of [3] with the automated attack generation capabilities of [4], and building upon the architectural advances of [1] and [2], could lead to more robust code agent systems. Future work should focus on developing standardized benchmarks for code agent security, improving red-teaming automation, and creating defensive mechanisms that protect code agents without sacrificing their functionality.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nCode agents represent a powerful paradigm for automated software development, but their increasing capabilities also introduce significant security and trustworthiness challenges. This paper has reviewed key advances in the field, including foundational deep learning architectures [1, 2], comprehensive trustworthiness assessment methodologies [3], and specialized red-teaming frameworks for code agents [4].\n\nOur analysis reveals that while significant progress has been made, substantial challenges remain. Current code agents exhibit vulnerabilities across multiple attack vectors, and existing red-teaming methodologies are not yet comprehensive enough to ensure robust safety. The specialized nature of code agent attack surfaces—particularly those involving code execution—demands continued research into domain-specific evaluation methodologies.\n\nFuture work should prioritize the development of standardized security benchmarks for code agents, the creation of more sophisticated red-teaming agents capable of discovering novel attack vectors, and the design of defensive mechanisms that can protect code agents in real-world deployment scenarios. By building on the foundations established by prior work in deep learning [1, 2], trustworthiness assessment [3], and automatic red-teaming [4], the research community can advance toward code agent systems that are both powerful and trustworthy.\n\n#